<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
	PHPUnit configuration for the Blitzy skin's own PHP test suites.

	WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
	=====================
	It does three jobs, and deliberately nothing else.

	  1. It declares the skin's three PHP suites, unit, integration and
	     structure, so that each one can be selected on its own. Gate 10
	     requires every step of the verification run to be individually
	     invocable for debugging, and a suite that cannot be named is a step
	     that cannot be debugged.

	  2. It defines the code coverage scope that rule R14's PHP floor is
	     measured against: includes/, and nothing else.

	  3. It sends that coverage into coverage/php/ as machine readable clover
	     XML and as an HTML report. Both are committed deliverables, which is
	     why .gitignore ignores caches and populated environment files but not
	     coverage/.

	Vector, the reference skin, ships no file like this one; it is run from
	the core tree under core's own configuration. Blitzy needs one because
	rule R14 states a line coverage floor, and a floor needs a measurement
	scope the skin owns rather than inherits. There is therefore no skin
	precedent to copy, and every choice below is justified against
	mediawiki/phpunit.xml.template instead. Where this file diverges from that
	template, the divergence is called out where it happens.

	A note on notation: PHPUnit's long command line options take two leading
	hyphens, but an XML comment may not contain two consecutive hyphens, so
	they are named below without them.

	HOW TO RUN IT
	=============
	PHPUnit resolves every relative path in a configuration file against the
	directory that file sits in, so this configuration works only when the
	package is at $IP/skins/Blitzy. Put it there physically, or bind mount it
	there, which is what harness/docker-compose.yml does. A symbolic link is
	not equivalent: POSIX resolves the `..` in the bootstrap path below
	through the link target, so on a symlinked package
	`../../tests/phpunit/bootstrap.php` points outside the core tree and the
	run dies before the first test. mediawiki/skins/README warns about the
	same behaviour, and it is one more reason the harness mounts rather than
	links.

	From the core tree, with $IP as the working directory:

	    Everything:  phpunit -c skins/Blitzy/phpunit.xml.dist
	    One suite:   the same, plus the long option `testsuite` set to
	                 `unit`, `integration` or `structure`
	    Faster run:  the same, plus the long option `no-coverage`, which
	                 skips the report block below

	PREREQUISITES
	=============
	  * The package at $IP/skins/Blitzy, as above.

	  * PHPUnit 9.6.34, the version MediaWiki development pins at both ends of
	    the range skin.json supports: the 1.43 LTS line the harness runs, and
	    the 1.47 development branch this repository checks out for API
	    reference. The 9.3 schema declared below is the one PHPUnit 9.6
	    validates against, and the `coverage` element used below is that
	    generation's replacement for the removed `filter`/`whitelist` form,
	    not an alias for it.

	  * A site settings file in the core tree. Core's bootstrap needs one even
	    for a unit only run, because it shells out to
	    tests/phpunit/getPHPUnitExtensionsAndSkins.php to learn which
	    extensions and skins to autoload, and that script loads settings.
	    harness/LocalSettings.template.php is where the verification instance
	    gets one. Note also that core's bootstrap only recognises its own
	    `core:unit` and `extensions:unit` suite names as settings free, so
	    selecting this file's `unit` suite still loads settings.

	  * On the 1.47 development branch only, core's bootstrap additionally
	    asserts that $IP/phpunit.xml exists and was generated by core, which
	    `composer phpunit:config` in the core tree does. MediaWiki 1.43 has no
	    such assertion, so the harness is unaffected.

	  * A coverage driver, either PCOV or Xdebug with coverage mode enabled.
	    The report block below makes every run of this configuration collect
	    coverage unless the `no-coverage` option is passed. The pinned
	    mediawiki:1.43.9 image ships no driver, and no Composer or PHPUnit
	    either, while harness/docker-compose.yml runs PHPUnit inside that
	    container because Gate 10's host contract allows only Docker and
	    Node.js on the host. Provisioning Composer, PHPUnit 9.6.34 and a
	    driver into that container is therefore harness work, not optional
	    tuning, and the failure mode is the quiet kind: with no driver PHPUnit
	    prints a warning, runs every test, writes no report and still exits
	    zero. failOnWarning does not catch it, because that flag governs test
	    warnings rather than the runner's own. A missing report must therefore
	    fail the R14 step described below, or a run with no coverage at all
	    reads as a passing one.

	THE R14 FLOOR, AND WHERE IT IS ENFORCED
	=======================================
	R14 puts the floor at 80% line coverage on the skin class and requires the
	report to come out of the single verification command. PHPUnit 9.6 has no
	minimum coverage option, so emitting a report is not the same as enforcing
	a threshold. The threshold is asserted by harness/verify.sh, which reads
	coverage/php/clover.xml and fails the run when the measured line rate
	falls below 80%. Naming that site here is the point: an enforcement step
	nobody can find is how a floor quietly becomes advisory. Clover carries a
	line rate per file as well as one for the project, so that step can assert
	the floor against includes/SkinBlitzy.php specifically, which is what R14
	names, rather than against an average the other three classes could carry.

	R14 also excludes Mustache templates, Less stylesheets and skin.json from
	coverage measurement, because Gate 9 verifies those three by asserting
	rendered output and delivered CSS instead, which is the only way their
	correctness is observable. The include list below selects .php files under
	includes/ only, so all three are excluded by construction rather than by
	an exclusion rule: includes/templates/*.mustache is excluded by suffix,
	and resources/**/*.less and skin.json are outside the included directory
	altogether. That is stated here explicitly so a reviewer can see the rule
	honoured instead of inferring it from an attribute.

	WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE
	=============================
	  * No fourth suite. R12 holds the surface to what the package actually
	    has, three directories under tests/phpunit/. Playwright owns the
	    browser specifications through playwright.config.ts and Karma owns the
	    client script suite through karma.conf.js; neither belongs here, and
	    there is no parser test or Selenium suite to add.

	  * No slow test detector extension. Core's template registers
	    Ergebnis\PHPUnit\SlowTestDetector\Extension, which is one of core's own
	    development dependencies. composer.json here does not declare it, and
	    R15 forbids depending on a package that is not declared and pinned.

	  * No coverage cache directory and no logging block. No rule or gate
	    needs either, and this file is easier to review without them.

	STRICTNESS
	==========
	The attribute block below is core's, taken whole rather than selectively,
	because Gate 2 requires a warning free build and these flags are what make
	a warning impossible to ignore. Four of them are worth a note of their own.

	forceCoversAnnotation makes PHPUnit refuse to attribute coverage to a test
	that does not declare what it covers. Core pairs it with the
	MediaWikiCoversValidator trait, which both MediaWikiUnitTestCase and
	MediaWikiIntegrationTestCase carry and which re-checks every test method by
	name, so a Blitzy test method without a valid @covers, or an explicit
	@coversNothing as core's own BundleSizeTestBase uses, fails the suite
	outright, whether or not that run is collecting coverage. When that happens
	the fix is the annotation. It is never the removal of this flag.

	failOnWarning and failOnRisky turn PHPUnit's softest signals into
	failures, and beStrictAboutTestsThatDoNotTestAnything and
	beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests are what classify a test that asserts
	nothing, or prints, as risky in the first place. All four together are how
	this suite stays honest about what it has actually proven.

	stderr sends PHPUnit's own output to standard error. Core does the same,
	for the same reason: an integration test renders real pages, and writing
	the progress report to standard output can leave MediaWiki believing
	headers have already been sent. Anything capturing this run has to merge
	the two streams, or it logs nothing.

	cacheResultFile keeps PHPUnit's result cache at
	Blitzy/.phpunit.result.cache, which .gitignore already ignores. PHPUnit
	would default to the same directory; naming it makes R1 checkable by
	reading this file rather than PHPUnit's source. Every other path here is
	relative to the package as well, and the core bootstrap is the only path
	that leaves it.
-->
<phpunit bootstrap="../../tests/phpunit/bootstrap.php"
		 xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
		 xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://schema.phpunit.de/9.3/phpunit.xsd"
		 cacheResultFile=".phpunit.result.cache"
		 colors="true"
		 backupGlobals="false"
		 convertDeprecationsToExceptions="true"
		 convertErrorsToExceptions="true"
		 convertNoticesToExceptions="true"
		 convertWarningsToExceptions="true"
		 forceCoversAnnotation="true"
		 failOnWarning="true"
		 failOnRisky="true"
		 stopOnFailure="false"
		 beStrictAboutTestsThatDoNotTestAnything="true"
		 beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests="true"
		 verbose="false"
		 printerClass="MediaWikiPHPUnitResultPrinter"
		 stderr="true">
	<!--
		Core's two values, kept for the reason core has them. An integration
		case builds a full service container and a coverage run carries the
		accumulated coverage data alongside it, so the ceiling that matters is
		not the one a given suite happens to need today. Both limits are
		removed rather than raised because a run killed by either surfaces as
		an error whose cause is not in the output, which is the worst kind of
		failure for a suite a harness runs unattended.
	-->
	<php>
		<ini name="memory_limit" value="-1" />
		<ini name="max_execution_time" value="0" />
	</php>
	<testsuites>
		<!--
			Unit tests: SkinBlitzyTest, BlitzyViewModelTest and
			BlitzyUrlValidatorTest. The directory is not a stylistic choice.
			MediaWikiUnitTestCase fails any test that is not located inside a
			directory named unit, so this suite points at tests/phpunit/unit
			and nowhere else, and nothing outside that directory may extend
			that base class.
		-->
		<testsuite name="unit">
			<directory>tests/phpunit/unit</directory>
		</testsuite>
		<!--
			Integration tests: SkinBlitzyIntegrationTest, BlitzyHooksTest and
			BlitzyConfigSecurityTest. Gate 13 pairs every registration with an
			invocation, and this is the suite that can honour it: the theme
			class the BeforePageDisplay handler puts on the html element is
			reachable from no template and from no unit test, so only a real
			page render observes it. That makes this suite a required step of
			harness/verify.sh rather than an optional one.
		-->
		<testsuite name="integration">
			<directory>tests/phpunit/integration</directory>
		</testsuite>
		<!--
			Structure tests: BundleSizeTest, which extends core's
			MediaWiki\Tests\Structure\BundleSizeTestBase and measures the
			modules capped in bundlesize.config.json. That base class exists
			only in the core tree, which is the other reason this
			configuration is designed to be run from there.
		-->
		<testsuite name="structure">
			<directory>tests/phpunit/structure</directory>
		</testsuite>
	</testsuites>
	<!--
		Core's convention: a case marked as belonging to the Broken group is a
		case core itself declines to run. No Blitzy group is invented here,
		because a group filter nobody applies is a filter that hides tests.
	-->
	<groups>
		<exclude>
			<group>Broken</group>
		</exclude>
	</groups>
	<!--
		includeUncoveredFiles is true, which is this file's one deliberate
		divergence from core's template, where it is false. Core's include
		list spans thousands of files, so counting untouched ones is
		expensive and noisy. This one holds four: SkinBlitzy,
		BlitzyViewModel, BlitzyUrlValidator and Hooks\BlitzyHooks. With the
		flag false, a class that no test loads is dropped from the report
		entirely and the percentage rises, so deleting a test file would
		improve the measured figure, and whether a class counts at all comes
		to depend on whether some unrelated test happened to load it. R14 is a
		floor, and a floor that rewards deleting tests is not one. True is
		also PHPUnit's own default, and with processUncoveredFiles left at
		false the untouched files are measured by static analysis rather than
		by being executed.
	-->
	<coverage includeUncoveredFiles="true">
		<!--
			R14's scope, in one line: production PHP, and nothing else. See
			the R14 section of the header for why this single entry is what
			excludes Mustache templates, Less stylesheets and skin.json.
		-->
		<include>
			<directory suffix=".php">includes</directory>
		</include>
		<!--
			Belt and braces. tests/ is already outside the included
			directory, so this entry removes nothing today; it is here so
			that broadening the include list later cannot silently start
			counting the tests as covered production code.
		-->
		<exclude>
			<directory suffix=".php">tests</directory>
		</exclude>
		<!--
			Two reports, for two audiences. clover.xml is what
			harness/verify.sh parses to assert the 80% floor, and the HTML
			report is what a reviewer reads; both live under coverage/php/,
			both are committed. lowUpperBound is moved from its default of 50
			to 80 so that the HTML report colours a file below R14's floor as
			low, instead of showing a failing file in the same band as a
			passing one.
		-->
		<report>
			<clover outputFile="coverage/php/clover.xml" />
			<html outputDirectory="coverage/php" lowUpperBound="80" />
		</report>
	</coverage>
	<!--
		Two of core's three extensions, and only these two. Both classes ship
		with core, in every release skin.json supports, and are registered in
		tests/Common/TestsAutoLoader.php, which the bootstrap above loads on
		both of its paths, so both resolve in the context this file runs from.

		The logger extension captures MediaWiki's own log output per test and
		the result printer named above appends it to a failure trace, which is
		the difference between a diagnosable integration failure and a stack
		trace. The teardown extension destroys the temporary test database
		after the last test; without it a repeated run accumulates cloned
		tables, and it is safe for a unit only run because the method it calls
		returns immediately when no test database was set up.

		Core's third extension is omitted for the reason given in the header.
	-->
	<extensions>
		<extension class="MediaWikiLoggerPHPUnitExtension" />
		<extension class="MediaWikiTeardownPHPUnitExtension" />
	</extensions>
</phpunit>
